### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is refuted on multiple grounds. The ACA's individual mandate penalty for a single individual was capped at the national average bronze plan premium, which ranged from approximately $2,484 to $3,816 depending on the year—far below the $10,000 figure cited ([KFF](https://www.kff.org/health-reform/issue-brief/the-cost-of-the-individual-mandate-penalty-for-the-remaining-uninsured/)). Additionally, the ACA included a specific affordability exemption for individuals for whom the lowest-cost bronze plan exceeded roughly 8–9.5% of household income, meaning someone who genuinely "couldn't afford" coverage would have been exempt, not penalized ([SHRM](https://www.shrm.org/topics-tools/news/benefits-compensation/affordable-care-act-coverage-terms)). Furthermore, the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 reduced the federal penalty to $0 beginning in 2019, so by October 2020 when this claim was made, no federal individual mandate penalty existed at all ([healthinsurance.org](https://www.healthinsurance.org/glossary/individual-mandate/), [Holland & Hart](https://www.hollandhart.com/2019-repeal-of-the-affordable-care-acts-individual-mandate-penalties)). The $10,000 figure is a significant exaggeration that misrepresents the penalty amount, ignores the affordability exemption, and is temporally inaccurate.